## 5. Key Terms

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Evaluation | judgement of the final product against success criteria |
| Reflection | thoughtful discussion of the development process and learning |
| Success criterion | measurable condition used to judge product success |
| Evidence | test results, screenshots, user feedback, or other proof |
| Client feedback | comments from the person/group who needed the solution |
| User feedback | comments from people who used or tested the product |
| Limitation | weakness, missing feature, or restriction in the final product |
| Improvement | realistic future change that would improve the product |
| Achieved | success criterion was fully met |
| Partly achieved | success criterion was partly met but has limits |
| Not achieved | success criterion was not met |
| Final judgement | overall conclusion about how successful the product is |
| Usability | how easy the product is for the user to use |
| Reliability | how consistently the product works correctly |
| Robustness | how well the product handles unexpected input or errors |
| Scope | what the project includes and excludes |
| User acceptance | whether the user can complete realistic tasks with the product |
| Test evidence | proof from testing that supports an evaluation claim |
| Future development | possible later work beyond the current IA |
| Justification | explanation that supports a judgement |
| Trade-off | design choice where one benefit is gained but another is limited |

---

## 6. What a Strong Evaluation Includes

A strong evaluation normally includes:

```text
success criteria table
reference to testing evidence
client/user feedback
judgement for each criterion
discussion of strengths
discussion of limitations
realistic improvements
overall conclusion
```

### Strong Evaluation Formula

```text
Criterion → Evidence → Judgement → Feedback → Limitation → Improvement
```

### Example

```text
SC4 was partly achieved. Test 8 shows that the weekly summary calculates missing homework totals correctly for one selected week. The client said the summary is useful, but they also wanted to compare several weeks at once. Therefore, a future improvement would be to add a date range comparison chart.
```

---

## 7. Evaluation Against Success Criteria

The easiest way to organize evaluation is by success criteria.

### Evaluation Table Template

| Success Criterion | Evidence | Achieved? | Evaluation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Add homework records | Test 1, Fig. 3 | Yes | The system saved valid records with all required fields. |
| Reject blank fields | Test 2, Fig. 4 | Yes | Blank required fields were rejected with an error message. |
| Search by student name | Test 5, Fig. 7 | Partly | Exact search worked, but partial spelling was not supported. |
| Weekly summary | Test 9, Fig. 10 | Yes | The summary count matched the manual expected total. |

### Why This Works

It shows:

```text
clear link to criteria
evidence
judgement
specific explanation
```

---

## 8. Achieved / Partly Achieved / Not Achieved

Use honest judgement.

### Achieved

Use when:

```text
feature works as described
testing evidence supports it
client/user can use it successfully
no major limitation affects the criterion
```

### Partly Achieved

Use when:

```text
feature works in some cases
feature works but has limitations
client/user needs more than current version provides
testing shows some weaknesses
```

### Not Achieved

Use when:

```text
feature was not completed
testing failed
feature does not match original criterion
no evidence supports it
```

### Important

It is better to honestly say “partly achieved” than to claim everything is perfect without evidence.

---

## 9. Using Testing Evidence

Evaluation should refer to testing.

### Weak Evaluation

```text
The search feature works well.
```

### Strong Evaluation

```text
The search feature meets Success Criterion 3 because Test 6 shows that entering an existing student name displays all matching records. Test 7 also shows that a non-existing name displays a clear "No records found" message.
```

### Good Evidence References

```text
Test 3
Figure 5
client feedback form
user testing note
screenshot of output
manual calculation comparison
```

### Key Rule

Every major evaluation claim should be supported by evidence.

---

## 10. Using Client/User Feedback

Client or user feedback helps show whether the product solves the real problem.

### Good Feedback Questions

```text
Can you complete the main task?
Which feature is most useful?
Which part is confusing?
Does the product solve the original problem?
What would you improve?
Is any important feature missing?
Would you use this instead of the old method?
```

### Weak Feedback

```text
The client said it was good.
```

### Strong Feedback

```text
The client said the search feature is useful because it reduces the time needed to find old records. However, they also said the weekly summary would be more helpful if it could compare multiple weeks.
```

---

## 11. Strengths

Strengths are the parts that worked well.

### Possible Strengths

```text
core features completed
clear user interface
data saved and loaded correctly
search works accurately
validation prevents bad input
summary calculation matches manual totals
client can complete main task
product reduces time compared with old method
```

### Strong Strength Statement

```text
One strength is that the system reduces manual searching. In the old paper method, the teacher had to check several pages, but the final product displays matching student records immediately after a search.
```

### Weak Strength Statement

```text
The system is very good.
```

Why weak?

```text
too vague
no evidence
no connection to problem
```

---

## 12. Limitations

Limitations are weaknesses or remaining restrictions.

### Possible Limitations

```text
search requires exact spelling
only one user role is supported
no cloud synchronization
cannot export PDF report
limited dataset size tested
interface is not optimized for mobile
no advanced login system
summary only covers one week at a time
data backup is manual
error messages could be clearer
```

### Good Limitation Statement

```text
A limitation is that the search function requires exact spelling. If the teacher mistypes a student's name, the system shows no results even when records exist. This limits usability for larger classes.
```

### Weak Limitation Statement

```text
The system could be better.
```

---

## 13. Future Improvements

Future improvements should be realistic and connected to limitations.

### Good Improvement

```text
Add partial search so the teacher can find records even if only part of the student's name is entered.
```

### Weak Improvement

```text
Make it better.
```

### Improvement Table

| Limitation | Future Improvement | Benefit |
|---|---|---|
| search requires exact spelling | add partial/fuzzy search | easier to find records |
| summary only one week | add date range comparison | better progress tracking |
| local data only | add secure backup/export | reduce data loss risk |
| no user roles | add teacher/admin roles | better access control |
| manual report sharing | add CSV export | easier sharing and analysis |

### Key Rule

Improvements should not be random new features. They should respond to real limitations or client feedback.

---

## 14. Reflection on Development

Reflection discusses the process, not only the final product.

### Reflection Can Discuss

```text
design changes
technical challenges
testing discoveries
client feedback influence
scope decisions
what was learned
what would be planned differently
trade-offs made
```

### Strong Reflection

```text
The original design used free text for completion status, but testing showed that different spellings such as "missing" and "Missing" caused summary errors. I changed this field to a dropdown list, which improved consistency and made the summary calculation more reliable.
```

### Weak Reflection

```text
I learned a lot and it was fun.
```

---

## 15. Design Changes in Evaluation

It is normal if the final product changed from the design.

### How to Explain

```text
Original design:
The status field was typed manually.

Problem found:
Users entered different spellings.

Change made:
The field was changed to a dropdown.

Impact:
This reduced data entry errors and improved summary accuracy.
```

### Why This Is Strong

It shows:

```text
testing or feedback influenced development
student made a reasoned design decision
final product improved
```

---

## 16. Evaluating Usability

Usability means whether users can use the product easily.

### Evidence for Usability

```text
client completed task without help
user feedback says navigation is clear
fewer steps than current method
clear labels and buttons
error messages are understandable
```

### Strong Usability Evaluation

```text
The interface is suitable for the client because the main tasks are available from one menu, and the client was able to add and search records during user testing without additional instructions.
```

### Weak Usability Evaluation

```text
The interface is easy.
```

---

## 17. Evaluating Reliability and Robustness

Reliability means the product works consistently.

Robustness means the product handles invalid input or problems safely.

### Evidence

```text
repeated tests pass
invalid input is rejected
empty search gives message
data loads after restart
error messages appear instead of crashing
```

### Example

```text
The product is reasonably robust for data entry because Tests 2, 3, and 4 show that blank fields, invalid scores, and missing dates are rejected with clear messages.
```

---

## 18. Evaluating Data Handling

If the product stores data, evaluation should discuss whether data handling works.

### Questions

```text
Is data saved correctly?
Can data be loaded again?
Is duplicate data avoided?
Can incorrect records be edited or deleted?
Is the data format suitable?
Is sensitive data protected enough for this project scope?
```

### Example

```text
Success Criterion 6 was achieved because Test 10 shows that records remained available after restarting the program. This means the file storage design meets the client's need to keep records between sessions.
```

---

## 19. Overall Final Judgement

At the end, write an overall judgement.

### Good Overall Judgement

```text
Overall, the product solves the client's main problem because the teacher can now add, search, and summarize homework records digitally instead of manually checking a paper notebook. Five of the six success criteria were fully achieved, while the search criterion was partly achieved because it requires exact spelling. The product is suitable for the client's current class, but future work should improve search flexibility and add export options.
```

### Weak Overall Judgement

```text
Overall, my IA was successful and everything worked well.
```

---

## 20. Evaluation Structure

A clear evaluation section may use:

```text
1. Evaluation against success criteria
2. Client/user feedback
3. Strengths of the final product
4. Limitations
5. Future improvements
6. Reflection on development process
7. Overall final judgement
```

### Shorter Structure

```text
Success criteria evaluation table
Client feedback summary
Limitations and improvements
Final conclusion
```

---

## 21. Example Success Criteria Evaluation

### Success Criterion 1

```text
The teacher can add a homework record with student name, task name, due date, and completion status.
```

### Evaluation

```text
This criterion was achieved. Test 1 shows that a valid homework record containing student name, task name, due date, and completion status was saved and displayed in the records table. The client also completed this task during user testing without extra help. This shows that the basic record entry feature meets the client's need to replace the paper notebook.
```

---

## 22. Example Partial Evaluation

### Success Criterion 3

```text
The teacher can search records by student name and view all matching records.
```

### Evaluation

```text
This criterion was partly achieved. Test 5 shows that searching an exact student name displays all matching records. However, Test 6 shows that a small spelling difference gives no result. The client said the search feature is still useful, but it would be better if partial names were accepted. A future improvement would be to add partial matching or case-insensitive search.
```

---

## 23. Example Not Achieved Evaluation

### Success Criterion 6

```text
The system can export a weekly report as a PDF.
```

### Evaluation

```text
This criterion was not achieved because the final product only displays the weekly report on screen and does not export a PDF file. This feature was removed from the final scope because the main record, search, and summary features took more development time than expected. A realistic future improvement would be to add CSV or PDF export after the core system is stable.
```

---

## 24. Example Client Feedback Summary

### Weak Version

```text
The client liked my system.
```

### Strong Version

```text
The client tested the final product using sample class data. They said the Add Record and Search features were useful because they reduce the need to manually check the paper notebook. They also suggested that the weekly summary should allow comparison across several weeks, which is not included in the current version. This feedback supports the evaluation that the product solves the main record-keeping problem but still has room for improvement in reporting.
```

---

## 25. Example Limitations and Improvements

| Limitation | Impact | Improvement |
|---|---|---|
| Search requires exact spelling | user may not find existing records after typo | add case-insensitive and partial search |
| Summary only shows one week | client cannot compare long-term trends | add date range summary |
| Data backup is manual | records may be lost if file is deleted | add automatic backup or export |
| Interface designed for laptop only | difficult on phone screen | add responsive layout |
| No role-based access | not suitable for multiple staff users | add login and access levels if needed |

